            'The inner oblique, middle circular and outer longitudinal muscle directions create movement of the contents in different directions. The thickening of the circular layer at the pylorus makes the sphincter; the sphincter is not the same structure as a mucosal fold. The vessels and nerve networks in the submucosa support the requirements of the inner secretory surface and the outer movement layers. Instead of memorising the layers as names, matching each with the task of protection, supply or movement is more lasting.',
